@pavelfeldman If I might ask, what is the motivation for using tuples?

Tuples as a data structure are pretty unintuitive - `Array<Array<string>>` sounds like a list of list of strings, and I had to actually read through the source-code to understand how that could possibly represent list of headers.

Even if you know what a tuple is (many developers don't) the type-hint does not reflect the fact that the inner list of strings is in fact a two-element tuple.

I would strongly suggest you use objects proper, e.g. `Array<Array<{ name: string, value: string }>>`, probably with the inner type defined separately and named `Header`.

Or at least use a tuple type, e.g. `Array<Array<{ [string, string] }>>`, which at least constraints the inner array to two elements - not that this helps with the semantic problem or makes the code very readable, as you will see still things like `header[0]` etc. in client code.

But I don't believe you need tuples in modern JS engines - the language is highly optimized for objects, both in terms of performance and memory usage. If this was done as a micro-optimization, it's an extremely marginal one, and probably not really worth the confusion it's going to cause.
